    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Avesta is the religious literature of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, composed in Avestan, and it holds the oldest texts in any Iranian language.</li><li>Its texts were handed down orally for many centuries, and were written down only from around the 5th or 6th century CE, in the Sasanian era.</li><li>Only part of the Sasanian edition survives, in ritual and devotional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, the oldest fragment dating to 1323 CE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Summaries in Zoroastrian texts of the 9th and 10th centuries indicate that only about one quarter of the Avestan sentences or verses they refer to can be found in the surviving texts.</p></aside>
    </section>

